Title: "Overview"
Id: "7ad33634-c9f0-482e-80da-39d9d7d70c23"

Text: "Type statements use `is`.

```mlg-fragment
X is \\set
f is \\function:on{A}:to{B}
A, B is \\set
```

Predicate-like type checks use `is?` and `is_not?`.

```mlg-fragment
x is? \\set
x is_not? \\empty.set
```

Builtin types start with two backslashes:

- `\\\\type` — the type of `Defines:` types
- `\\\\statement`, `\\\\expression`, `\\\\specification` — the types of statements,
  expressions, and specification literals
- `\\\\opaque` — an unstructured value
- `\\\\abstract` — the abstract `:->` capability target

The right-hand side of `is` may also be a **structural type literal** rather
than a nominal command type. These use complete spec literals at every leaf:

```mlg-fragment
(x, y) is (? is \\natural, ? \"in\" \\reals)
{x : ...} is {? is \\natural : ...}
f is (? is \\natural) -> (? \"in\" \\naturals)
f is (? is \\natural, ? \"in\" \\reals) -> (? is \\real)
```

A raw nominal tuple such as `(\\natural, \\real)` is not a type literal; write
`(? is \\natural, ? is \\real)` instead. The `->` form takes one or more input
spec literals and exactly one output spec literal, and the declared input arity
is preserved rather than being curried into nested unary types."
Id: "2f73683b-6420-4fe6-8196-3ce83be64464"
